I: Hi! Thanks for taking part in this short 10-minute interview. We're talking about a new online platform for students to discover and join campus clubs. Just share your honest thoughts, there’s no right or wrong answers. Shall we start?
T: Sure, sounds good. I’m happy to help out.
I: Wonderful, let’s get started.
Hypothesis 1: Smart Discovery (Filters)

I: First, imagine this website lets you filter clubs by interest, time commitment, and difficulty level to find suitable matches instantly. Could you walk me through how you would use these filters to search for a club?
T: I’d start by locking in my available time first because my study schedule is pretty fixed. After setting my weekly free time range, I’d browse interest categories that I want to explore, not just my existing hobbies. Finally, I’ll check difficulty to pick beginner-friendly options so I won’t feel pressured as a new member.
I: Which of the three filters is most important to you? Please explain your answer.
T: Difficulty level is my top priority. I'm a total beginner in most hobbies, so I always worry about joining clubs that require advanced skills. If a club is too demanding, I’ll feel left out and quit quickly, no matter how interesting it is.
I: What website details would make this filtering process feel effortless and easy to use?
T: I love one-click tag filters and clear category divisions. It would be great if the page could hide unsuitable results automatically and let me save my filter settings for next time, so I don’t have to reset everything repeatedly.
I: Even with these filters, is there anything that would still make you feel unsure or confused during your search?
T: Yeah, I think the lack of standard definitions would be confusing. For example, “low time commitment” could mean one hour a week for one person and three hours for another, which makes filtering less accurate.
I: Apart from filters, what other key information do you need to confirm a club is a good fit?
T: I need to know the group size, whether it's a small tight team or a large casual group, and if the club has mandatory events that I can’t miss.
I: Are there any extra filters you wish the website offered to find clubs faster?
T: I hope there's a filter for semester-long vs short-term clubs, and also a filter for no-experience required, that would save me a lot of time.
Hypothesis 2: Social-driven Features

I: Next scenario: the platform shows your friends’ updates, like which clubs they’ve joined or shown interest in. How much would this influence you to check out a new club?
T: It would moderately influence me. I won’t jump in right away, but I will be curious and click to learn more. Social recommendations make a club feel more trustworthy than random suggestions.
I: Would you join a club just because your friends are in it? Why or why not?
T: Not solely for that reason. I still need to be mildly interested in the club’s content. But if I’m on the fence about a club, having friends there will definitely push me to join.
I: What social features would make you want to use this club website more regularly?
T: I’d like a feature to see what clubs are trending among students in my major, and a simple follow function for clubs I’m interested in. It makes the platform more interactive instead of just a static search page.
I: Would seeing friends’ club activity encourage you to try clubs you’d normally avoid?
T: Definitely. I’m quite conservative and stick to familiar activities. Seeing peers try new things would motivate me to step out of my comfort zone.
I: What other social functions would make joining clubs more fun on this platform?
T: A simple Q&A section where current members answer questions, and a feature to see upcoming social mixers for new members.
Hypothesis 3: Clear Club Information

I: Moving on. Every club profile clearly shows daily activities, meeting times, requirements and real member life. What’s the most important information for you before joining?
T: Meeting frequency and mandatory obligations. I need to know if there are extra tasks, competitions or assignments I have to complete as a member.
I: Do you think current club information is clear and well-organized? What’s missing or confusing?
T: Not at all. Most club introductions are overly promotional. They only talk about fun parts and hide downsides, like heavy workloads or strict attendance rules, which is really misleading.
I: How would clear, organized information help you make faster joining decisions?
T: I can compare different clubs at a glance. I won’t waste time messaging club leaders to ask basic questions, and I can quickly rule out clubs that don’t match my limits.
I: What club culture or community details should always be included on every profile?
T: I want to know if the community is relaxed and flexible, or very strict and formal. Also, whether leaders are friendly and supportive to new students.
I: What specific profile details would make you 100% sure a club is right for you?
T: Honest reviews from current members, flexible attendance policies, detailed weekly activity plans, and clear notes on any extra costs or commitments.
Hypothesis 4: Easy Joining Process

I: Last part. The website has a simple step-by-step online joining process, and you can finish the application in minutes. How would this affect your willingness to join clubs?
T: It would boost my willingness a lot. Long, complicated application forms are a huge turn-off. A quick and easy process removes the hassle and makes me more willing to give new clubs a try.
I: In this ideal easy process, what’s the minimum steps and information needed to join?
T: 2 to 3 steps is ideal. Just basic contact info, student ID, and a short sentence about why I want to join. No long essays or unnecessary personal details.
I: Are there any traditional joining steps you want to remove completely? Like paperwork or long waiting periods?
T: I really hope to get rid of offline sign-up sheets and long manual approval waits. Instant online submission and automatic confirmation would be way more convenient.
I: Even with a simple process, what might stop you from finishing your application?
T: Complicated page layout, unexpected pop-ups, or forced account linking. Small, annoying design issues always make me quit halfway.
I: How would you design a perfect, quick and enjoyable joining experience?
T: A clean one-page application, clear progress bar, simple terms and conditions, and an immediate confirmation message. Keep it straightforward and stress-free.
 Closing

I: Alright, we’ve finished all the core questions. Thank you so much for sharing your unique thoughts on filters, social features, club information and the sign-up process. Your feedback is super valuable for our project.
T: It’s my pleasure. It was interesting to think about how to improve club platforms.
I: That’s great to hear. Do you have any additional comments or suggestions about finding and joining campus clubs that we didn’t cover today?
T: Not really, I think we covered all the key areas I care about.
I: Perfect. Thanks again for your time and honest participation. Have a nice day!
T: You too, thank you!